        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A ceiling fan whose motor runs but whose light will not come on is telling you something useful: power is reaching the fan. That rules out the breaker, most of the house wiring and the motor in one go. What is left is a short chain of parts that only the light depends on, from the bulbs to a hidden wattage limiter, the pull chain, the remote receiver, the switch leg and the light kit itself. Work down that chain in order, cheapest and most likely first, and most people find the fault in under half an hour.
        </p>

        <div id="start-here" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Start Here: What a Dead Light Tells You
          </h2>

          <p>
            The light in a ceiling fan is fed through a short, separate path inside the fan. In a typical fan, house power arrives at the canopy, splits, and the light side passes through either a pull chain switch or a remote receiver, then through a wattage limiter, then through a plug connector into the light kit and finally to the sockets or the LED board. A failure anywhere along that path kills the light while leaving the motor untouched. Your job is simply to find which link broke.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to how the light failed. A light that died suddenly after a bulb change points to the bulbs or the limiter. One that flickered for weeks before going out suggests a loose connection or a failing LED driver. A light that stopped responding to the remote while the fan still obeys it points at the receiver or the pairing. And a light that works from the pull chain but not the wall switch is a wiring question, not a parts question. Write down the history before you start.
          </p>

          <p>
            The table below maps the common symptoms to their usual causes. It is a starting point rather than a verdict, but in our experience it is right more often than not, and it stops you from pulling the canopy down to chase a wiring fault when the real culprit is a bulb that is one watt too many for the limiter. If you are still choosing a fan, our <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">ceiling fans with lights buying guide</a> covers which light kits are easiest to live with.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
