        <div id="bulbs-limiter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Bulbs and the 190-Watt Limiter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the bulbs, even if they look fine, because a fan light kit puts bulbs through vibration and heat that a table lamp never sees. Filaments in old incandescent bulbs break from fan shake, and cheap LED bulbs fail early when they are sealed in an enclosed glass bowl that traps heat. Do not test one bulb at a time in the fan; take a bulb you know works from a lamp elsewhere in the house and put it in the fan. If the light comes on, you are finished and can buy replacements.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you recently changed bulbs and the light quit immediately, suspect the wattage limiter. Under the Energy Policy Act of 2005, the Department of Energy requires ceiling fan light kits made or imported since January 1, 2009 to limit the light kit to 190 watts in total. Manufacturers meet that with a small current-limiting device, often a module in the switch housing. If the installed bulbs draw more than the limit, the light simply will not operate, and some limiters cut out and reset rather than staying off.
          </p>

          <p>
            This mostly catches people who put incandescent or halogen bulbs back into a fan that was designed around lower-wattage lamps. Four 60-watt incandescent bulbs total 240 watts, comfortably over the 190-watt line, and the light goes dark even though every bulb is good. The fix is not to bypass the limiter; it is to switch to LED bulbs, which draw a fraction of the power for the same light. A typical 60-watt-equivalent LED pulls around 8 to 10 watts, so even a five-light kit stays far below the cap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the bulb type as well as the wattage. Many fans use candelabra (E12) sockets rather than standard medium (E26) sockets, and a bulb that is nearly the right size can sit in the socket without its base touching the center contact. Look at the maximum wattage printed inside each socket or on the light kit label, and respect it even when using LED bulbs, because the per-socket rating exists for heat as well as current. If the fan has a dimmable remote, the bulbs must also be dimmable, which we come back to below.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never bypass the wattage limiter.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">It is there because federal rules require it and because light kits are not built for the heat of 240 watts of incandescent bulbs. If the limiter trips, the answer is lower-wattage bulbs, which in practice means LEDs. A limiter that trips on a handful of LEDs is itself faulty and should be replaced with the manufacturer's part.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            One more bulb-related trap: the socket tab. At the bottom of each socket is a small brass tab that presses against the bulb's base. Over years of overtightened bulbs, that tab gets flattened and stops making contact. With the breaker off, gently lift it about an eighth of an inch with a small flat screwdriver, then try the bulb again. It is a two-minute fix that solves a surprising share of lights that seem dead for no reason, and it costs nothing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pull-chain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Pull Chain Switch
          </h2>

          <p>
            On fans without a remote, the light is usually controlled by its own pull chain, separate from the fan speed chain. That switch is a small rotary mechanism that clicks between on and off each time the chain is pulled, and it is one of the most common failure points on older fans. The internal contacts wear, the spring weakens, or the chain snaps off inside the housing. When it fails, it can fail stuck off, stuck on, or intermittent, which is the most confusing of the three.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test it simply. With the wall switch on and known-good bulbs in place, pull the light chain slowly several times and listen. A healthy switch gives a crisp click every pull and the light alternates on and off. A switch that clicks but never lights, or one that only lights on every third or fourth pull, is worn inside. If the chain has broken off flush with the housing, you can sometimes grab the stub with needle-nose pliers, but the switch usually needs replacing anyway once that happens.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacing a light pull chain switch is a manageable job for a careful homeowner. Turn the breaker off, not just the wall switch, and confirm the light kit is dead with a non-contact voltage tester. Remove the switch housing cover, photograph the wiring, and note which wires go to the old switch. A light switch is normally a simple two-wire on and off type, while the fan speed switch has more terminals, so make sure you buy the right one. Match the replacement to the old switch's markings.
          </p>

          <p>
            A quick note for fans that have both a remote and pull chains. Many remote kits require the light chain to be left in the on position, because the receiver now does the switching. If someone pulled that chain to off, the remote will appear to have failed. Before you buy a new remote or receiver, pull the light chain once, then try the remote again. It sounds too simple to matter, yet it is one of the most frequent reasons a remote-controlled fan light stops responding after a cleaning.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="remote-receiver" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Remote, Receiver, Dip Switches and Pairing
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your fan is remote controlled, the light runs through a receiver tucked inside the canopy or the switch housing. The receiver takes house power, listens for the remote's signal, and switches or dims the fan and light separately. That means there are three parts that can fail on the remote side: the handset itself, the link between handset and receiver, and the receiver's light output. Start with the cheapest. Replace the remote's battery, which is often a 12-volt A23 or a coin cell, even if the indicator still blinks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, check the link. Older remotes, and many replacement universal kits, use dip switches, a row of tiny sliders in the battery compartment that must match a matching row on the receiver. If a battery change or a drop has bumped one slider, the remote talks on a different code and the fan ignores it. Newer fans mostly pair instead: you cut power at the breaker or wall switch, restore it, and press a button on the remote within a short window, commonly 30 to 60 seconds. Your manual gives the exact sequence.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the fan responds to the remote but the light does not, the link is fine and the problem is the light side. The most common reason is the bulb. Remotes that dim the light send a chopped signal that non-dimmable LED bulbs handle badly, so they may flicker, buzz, glow faintly or stay off. Fit dimmable LED bulbs and try again. If that does not help and you have confirmed good bulbs and a good pull chain position, the receiver's light channel has likely failed, which is a known weak point on budget kits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Receivers live in a hot, cramped space above the fan, and heat is their main enemy. A receiver that has worked for years and then quits, especially after a hot summer, is a prime suspect. Most are a small boxed module with plug-in or wire-nut connections and are replaceable on their own. Buy the manufacturer's matching kit where possible. A universal kit can work, but check that it is rated for the fan's motor and light load and that it fits in the canopy before you take the old one out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check for a dimmer on the wall. A remote receiver should be fed by a plain on and off switch left in the on position. A wall dimmer or a fan speed control upstream of the receiver starves it of clean power, which can make the light behave erratically and can damage the receiver over time. If you find a dimmer controlling a remote-equipped fan, have it replaced with a standard switch. This also matters for DC motor fans, which we cover in our <a href="/dc-vs-ac-ceiling-fan/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">DC vs AC ceiling fan comparison</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="switch-wiring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Wall Switch, the Blue Wire and the Switch Leg
          </h2>

          <p>
            When the light worked at some point and stopped, wiring is low on the list. When it has never worked properly since installation, wiring moves to the top. Fans typically arrive with a black wire for the motor and a blue wire for the light, plus white for neutral and green or bare for ground. How those are connected in the ceiling box decides whether the light gets power at all, and a surprising number of fans are installed with the blue wire capped off on its own and connected to nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the ceiling box has one switched hot conductor, usually black, the standard approach is to connect both the fan's black and blue wires to it, so the wall switch powers both and the chains or remote control them separately. If the box has a second switched conductor, often a red wire from a three-conductor cable, it was likely run so the light and fan could have their own wall switches. In that case blue goes to red and black goes to black, and each wall switch controls one function. Check both switches.
          </p>

          <p>
            That second switch is the hidden cause behind many dead fan lights. Someone installed the fan with the light on the red switch leg, and the second wall switch has since been forgotten, painted over or swapped for a blank plate. Before opening anything, look for an unlabeled switch near the room entrance or a switch that seems to do nothing. Flip it with the light chain on. If the light comes on, nothing is broken; the circuit was simply designed for separate control.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do need to open the ceiling box, the breaker must be off and verified off. A wall switch only interrupts one conductor, and boxes often contain wires from other circuits. Our guide to the <a href="/best-circuit-breaker-finder/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best circuit breaker finders</a> covers the tools that identify the right breaker quickly in a panel with poor labels. Once the canopy is lowered, look for wire connectors that have loosened, a blue wire capped alone, or copper that has scorched or discolored. Any sign of heat damage is a reason to stop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="light-kit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Loose Light Kit Plugs and Integrated LED Failures
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most fans connect the light kit to the motor housing through a plug-in connector, usually a small two- or three-pin plug near the switch housing. The plug makes installation easier, but it lives right next to a vibrating motor, and over years the connection can work loose. A loose plug produces exactly the pattern of a light that flickers when the fan runs, works when you tap the housing, then eventually goes dark. It is also one of the easiest faults to fix once you know where to look.
          </p>

          <p>
            With the breaker off, remove the glass and the light kit cover, then lower or unscrew the light kit from the switch housing according to the manual. Find the connector, unplug it, look for bent, burnt or corroded pins, and push it back together firmly until it latches. Check any wire connectors in the light kit for loose conductors at the same time. If the fan wobbles noticeably, fix that too, because excessive shake is what loosens these plugs in the first place, as our guide to <a href="/why-is-my-ceiling-fan-wobbling/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">why a ceiling fan wobbles</a> explains.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fans with integrated LED lights are a different case. There are no bulbs to change, and the light is a circuit board of LEDs fed by a small driver that converts house power to the low voltage the LEDs need. When an integrated light dies while the fan keeps working, the failure is usually the driver or the LED board, not the fan. Some go out abruptly, while others dim unevenly, flicker at low settings or develop a dark patch where a string of LEDs has failed.
          </p>

          <p>
            The repair route for integrated LEDs depends entirely on the manufacturer. Many fans have a replacement LED module available as a part, often a plug-in board and driver that swap in without tools beyond a screwdriver. Others are not sold separately, and the only factory fix is a new light kit or a new fan. Before buying anything, find the model number on the label on top of the motor housing and check the manufacturer's parts page or contact support. Warranties on integrated lights are sometimes longer than you would expect.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="call-a-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Safety, and When to Call an Electrician
          </h2>

          <p>
            Everything in this guide that goes beyond bulbs, chains and remotes involves exposed wiring, so do it with the breaker off. Turning off the wall switch is not enough. A switch interrupts only the hot conductor it controls, a fan may be fed from a box that contains other live circuits, and a fan with a separate light switch leg may have one wire dead and another live. Turn off the breaker, then confirm with a non-contact voltage tester on every conductor before you touch anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stop and call a licensed electrician when you find any scorching, melted insulation or a burnt smell, when the box has more wires than you can identify confidently, when you see aluminum branch wiring, or when breakers trip as soon as the light is switched on. Call one too if the fan was installed without a fan-rated box, because that is a safety issue for the whole fan, not just the light. Electricians handle these quickly, and the cost is small next to the risk of guessing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sometimes the most sensible fix is a new fan. If the motor is noisy or slow, the receiver has already been replaced once, the integrated light kit is no longer sold, or the fan predates current efficiency standards, the money you would put into parts goes a long way toward a better fan. Our <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">ceiling fans with lights guide</a> ranks the fans we tested, and our <a href="/what-size-ceiling-fan-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">ceiling fan sizing guide</a> makes sure the replacement suits the room.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">190 W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Federal cap on fan light kit wattage</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8-10 W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical 60 W-equivalent LED bulb</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30-60 s</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common remote pairing window</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">15-30 lb</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical full-size fan weight</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Most of the time, though, you will not get this far. In our experience the large majority of dead fan lights are bulbs, a tripped limiter after the wrong bulbs went in, a pull chain left in the off position, or a remote that needs a battery and a re-pair. Work through the list in order, change one thing at a time, and test after each change. That discipline turns an annoying evening of guesswork into a quick, cheap repair, and it tells you exactly what to replace if a part has truly failed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing with the bulbs you just removed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you pull a bulb out of the fan and put it back, you learn nothing. Always test with a bulb you know works from another fixture. Fan bulbs fail more often than lamp bulbs because of vibration and trapped heat, so assume nothing about bulbs that have lived in the fan for a while.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting incandescent bulbs back in</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Old-style bulbs can push the light kit past the federal 190-watt limit, and the limiter shuts the light off even though every bulb is good. People then chase a wiring fault that does not exist. Use LED bulbs of the correct base and the light kit will stay well under the cap.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Turning off the wall switch instead of the breaker</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wall switch only breaks one conductor, and fans with separate light and fan switch legs can have a live wire even with one switch off. Ceiling boxes also carry other circuits. Turn the breaker off and check every wire with a non-contact tester before you touch anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using non-dimmable bulbs with a dimming remote</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Remote receivers that dim the light do not play well with non-dimmable LED bulbs. The light may flicker, buzz, glow faintly or stay off, and it looks exactly like a failed receiver. Check the bulb packaging before you buy a new remote kit you do not actually need.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving a dimmer on a remote fan circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A wall dimmer or speed control feeding a remote receiver starves it of clean power and shortens its life. The receiver should get full power from a plain on and off switch. If an old dimmer is still on the wall, swap it for a standard switch before replacing anything else.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my ceiling fan work but the light doesn't?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because the light runs through its own small circuit inside the fan. Power clearly reaches the canopy if the motor spins, so the fault is in the parts only the light uses: bulbs, socket tabs, the wattage limiter, the light pull chain, the remote receiver's light channel, the plug to the light kit or an integrated LED driver. Check them in that order.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I reset a ceiling fan light?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most fans have no reset button for the light. Cutting power at the breaker or wall switch for about 30 seconds resets a remote receiver and some wattage limiters, and on many remote fans you then re-pair within a short window after restoring power. If the limiter tripped because of high-wattage bulbs, it will trip again until you fit LEDs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the 190-watt limit on ceiling fan lights?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Federal rules from the Energy Policy Act of 2005 require ceiling fan light kits made or imported since 2009 to limit total light kit wattage to 190 watts. Manufacturers use a current-limiting device, and if the bulbs draw more, the light will not run. Switching to LED bulbs, which draw a fraction of the power, solves it every time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I replace just the light pull chain switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. The light switch is usually a simple two-wire on and off switch, separate from the multi-terminal fan speed switch. With the breaker off, photograph the wiring, match the replacement to the old switch's markings, connect the leads one at a time and secure it in the housing. Stop if the wiring does not match what you expected.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why did my ceiling fan light stop working with the remote?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with a fresh battery, then re-pair or check the dip switches match. If the fan obeys the remote but the light does not, check that the bulbs are dimmable and the light pull chain is in the on position. If both are fine, the receiver's light channel has likely failed and the receiver needs replacing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does the blue wire on a ceiling fan do?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On most fans the blue wire feeds the light kit and the black wire feeds the motor. With one switched hot in the ceiling box, both connect to it. With two, typically black and red from a three-conductor cable, blue usually goes to red so a second wall switch controls the light. A capped-off blue wire means the light gets no power.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an integrated LED ceiling fan light be repaired?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes. Many manufacturers sell the LED board and driver as a plug-in replacement part. Find the model number on the motor housing label and check the manufacturer's parts page or support line. If the module is no longer available, a compatible bulb-based light kit or a new fan are the remaining options, depending on the fan's age.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

