        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A ceiling fan light that will not come on is almost never a mystery once you follow the circuit. Bulbs and socket tabs first, then the wattage limiter, the pull chain, the remote and receiver, the switch leg, and finally the light kit plug or LED module. Change one thing at a time and test after each step.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Do the electrical work with the breaker off and verified dead, stop at any sign of heat damage, and do not be shy about calling an electrician. And if the fan is old, noisy and now needs parts, a replacement is often the better spend than a repair.
          </p>
